This version uses recursion to place each node in the list for its depth. The first time a depth appears, a new level is created. Values at odd depths are inserted at the front, which gives the reverse direction without changing the tree.

A dry run with values `1, 2, 3` gives depth 0 as `[1]`, then depth 1 as `[3, 2]`. The recursion visits children in normal left then right order, while insertion decides the final order.

```go
/*
type TreeNode struct {
    Val   int
    Left  *TreeNode
    Right *TreeNode
}
*/

func zigzagLevelOrder(root *TreeNode) [][]int {
    answer := make([][]int, 0)

    var visit func(*TreeNode, int)
    visit = func(node *TreeNode, depth int) {
        if node == nil {
            return
        }

        if depth == len(answer) {
            answer = append(answer, []int{})
        }

        if depth%2 == 0 {
            answer[depth] = append(answer[depth], node.Val)
        } else {
            answer[depth] = append([]int{node.Val}, answer[depth]...)
        }

        visit(node.Left, depth+1)
        visit(node.Right, depth+1)
    }

    visit(root, 0)
    return answer
}
```

Each node is visited once, so the time complexity is `O(n)`. The recursion uses `O(h)` call stack space, while the output stores `O(n)` values.

Two stacks make the direction change easy to see. One stack holds the current level. While removing a node, its children are pushed into the other stack in an order that matches the next direction.

The empty root case is checked before using either stack. This method does not modify any tree nodes.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int> > zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<vector<int> > answer;
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return answer;
        }

        stack<TreeNode*> current;
        stack<TreeNode*> next;
        current.push(root);
        bool leftToRight = true;

        while (!current.empty()) {
            vector<int> level;

            while (!current.empty()) {
                TreeNode* node = current.top();
                current.pop();
                level.push_back(node->data);

                if (leftToRight) {
                    if (node->left != nullptr) {
                        next.push(node->left);
                    }
                    if (node->right != nullptr) {
                        next.push(node->right);
                    }
                } else {
                    if (node->right != nullptr) {
                        next.push(node->right);
                    }
                    if (node->left != nullptr) {
                        next.push(node->left);
                    }
                }
            }

            answer.push_back(level);
            swap(current, next);
            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity is `O(n)`. The two stacks hold at most one tree level, so the extra space is `O(n)`, not counting the returned result.

The queue still stores the next level, but the current level is filled from opposite ends of an array list. This avoids reversing the finished list. On a left to right level, values go at indexes `0, 1, 2`; on the next level, they go at the end toward the front.

Dry run for a small tree:

```text
    1
   / \
  2   3
 / \
4   5
```

The first level is `[1]`, the second is `[2, 3]`, and the third is `[5, 4]`.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> zigzagLevelOrder(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }

        Deque<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);
        boolean leftToRight = true;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int size = queue.size();
            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                level.add(0);
            }

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                TreeNode node = queue.poll();
                int index = leftToRight ? i : size - 1 - i;
                level.set(index, node.data);

                if (node.left != null) {
                    queue.offer(node.left);
                }
                if (node.right != null) {
                    queue.offer(node.right);
                }
            }

            answer.add(level);
            leftToRight = !leftToRight;
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The traversal takes `O(n)` time. The queue and output use `O(n)` space.

## Intuition
The question is *very similar to the level order traversal* of the binary tree. If we closely observe, then we find that **element in the even level are in reverse order** whereas the **order of elements in the odd level remain the same.**



## Approach
1. The entire approach is very similar to the level order traversal of the binary tree with some changes or updation.
2. First take a flag variable (initially initialized to 1).
3. Before adding the vector, check if the flag is even, then reverse the currentLevel vector using: reverse(currentLevel.begin(),currentLevel.end()).
4. After each addition of the currentLevel vector in the result, increment the flag variable.

